<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Rudolf Slánský</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1901">1901</a>–<a href="/years/1952">1952</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Rudolf Slánský was General Secretary of the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> from 1945 to 1951 and a leader of the 1948 takeover. Arrested in 1951, he was the chief defendant in a Stalinist show trial in November 1952 with strongly antisemitic overtones, after which he and ten co-defendants were hanged. He was rehabilitated in the 1960s.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Rudolf Slánský was born on 31 July 1901 at Nezvěstice into a conservative Jewish family and attended the Commercial Academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plzen">Plzeň</a>. After the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> he went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, where he found a leftist intellectual scene, and in 1921 he joined the Communist Party of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> when it broke away from the Social Democratic Party. He became a senior lieutenant of its leader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klement-gottwald">Klement Gottwald</a> and in 1929 a member of the Presidium and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/politburo">Politburo</a>. He lived in hiding from 1929 to 1935 because the party was illegal, and was elected to the National Assembly in 1935.</p><p>After Nazi Germany occupied the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudetenland">Sudetenland</a> in October 1938 he fled to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a> with much of the party leadership. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> he worked on broadcasts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a> and organised Czechoslovak army units, with which he returned in 1944 to take part in the Slovak National Uprising. His time in Moscow brought him into contact with the often brutal methods Soviet communists used to maintain party discipline.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>After the war Slánský helped organise the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-front-czechoslovakia">National Front</a> government under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edvard-benes">Edvard Beneš</a>, and he became General Secretary of the party, second only to Gottwald. After the communist seizure of power in February 1948 he was the second most powerful man in the country. He was blamed for economic and industrial troubles, which cost him popular support, but he received the Order of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/socialism">Socialism</a> on 30 July 1951.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/josip-broz-tito">Tito</a> broke with Stalin, Stalin decided to purge the communist parties of the satellite states. Most historians think that Gottwald sacrificed his friend to save himself; others point to a rivalry that grew after 1948, in which Stalin backed Gottwald. Slánský was arrested at 1 a.m. on 24 November 1951, one of 14 leaders arrested, and was tortured into confessing and attempted suicide. The party said he was spying for an international Western capitalist conspiracy.</p><p>The trial began on 20 November 1952 before the Senate of the State Court, with Josef Urválek as prosecutor, and lasted eight days. As in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> show trials of the late 1930s, the defendants admitted guilt and requested a death sentence. Slánský was found guilty of Trotskyite-Titoist-Zionist activities in the service of American imperialism, and was hanged at Pankrác Prison on 3 December 1952 with ten others. His ashes were scattered on an icy road outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>. He and the other victims were cleared in April 1963 and fully rehabilitated in May 1968. He was the most powerful politician executed under communist rule in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>, and afterwards fallen leaders were stripped of power and told to retire.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Rudolf Slánský” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Thereafter the party ruled through its Central Committee and the security police, staging show trials such as that of <b>Rudolf Slánský</b> in 1952.</blockquote><a href="/empire/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Nezvěstice, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prague">Prague</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/czech-republic">the Czech Republic</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.64,13.52&amp;year=1934" aria-label="Nezvěstice on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Rudolf Slánský"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5742 5805h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5742 5805h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.64,13.52&amp;year=1934">What was here in 1934? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
